Key requirements include the disclosure of non-financial information for large companies, addressing environmental, social, and governance (ESG) issues. The Act requires these entities to report on how their operations impact the environment, human rights, and working conditions, alongside efforts to combat corruption. The law also outlines standards for accounting practices, valuation of assets and liabilities, and presentation of financial statements, ensuring comparability and consistency.', 'The Norwegian Accounting Act, enacted in 1998, establishes a framework for financial reporting and accounting practices for entities operating in Norway. It mandates the preparation of annual financial statements and management reports that provide a true and fair view of an entity’s financial position and performance. The Act aligns with EU directives and incorporates provisions for sustainability-related disclosures, emphasizing transparency and accountability in corporate reporting.

